{#-
  listener_report.txt.j2

  Rendered TWICE from the same data by playbooks/linux/listener_sweep.yml:

    report_mask: true    -> the distribution copy, addresses masked
    report_mask: false   -> the archive copy, mode 0600, full addresses

  ONE TEMPLATE, NOT TWO. Separate masked and unmasked templates drift, and
  the two drifts are not equally visible: a field added to the archive copy
  and forgotten in the masked one is a MISSING FIELD, which somebody
  notices; a field added to the masked one without its mask is a LEAK, which
  nobody does. Branching inside one template means every value renders
  through the same code path and the mask applies by construction.

  EVERYTHING THAT CAN CARRY AN ADDRESS GOES THROUGH addr(). That includes
  the check name and the actual/expected fields, because this playbook bakes
  proto/address:port into all three. A summary section rendered from
  pre-joined strings would bypass the macro entirely -- which is exactly the
  leak that showed up in service_health.txt.j2 and is why both the per-host
  rows and the summary sections below iterate host_rows.

  INPUT: host_rows, list of dicts. The template never touches hostvars, so
  tests can drive it from fixtures with no hosts present.

      name       str   host identifier
      reachable  bool  false -> no checks at all, never zeroed counts
      verdict    str   PASS | FAIL | SKIP
      checks     list  of check / status / expected / actual / reason
      reason     str   present only when reachable is false
-#}
{%- macro hostname(value) -%}
{%- if value is not defined or value in (none, '') -%}
-
{%- elif report_mask -%}
{{ value | mask_hostname }}
{%- else -%}
{{ value }}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
{%- macro addr(value) -%}
{#- Socket strings are proto/address:port. mask_ip RAISES on input it cannot
    parse, so the address is extracted and masked while the surrounding
    structure is preserved. A wildcard bind is not an address and is left
    alone: masking 0.0.0.0 would hide the single most important fact in the
    report. -#}
{%- if value is not defined or value in (none, '') -%}
-
{%- elif not report_mask -%}
{{ value }}
{%- else -%}
{%- set parts = value.split('/') -%}
{%- if parts | length < 2 -%}
{{ value }}
{%- else -%}
{%- set rest = parts[1:] | join('/') -%}
{%- set port = rest.split(':') | last -%}
{%- set a = rest[:(rest | length) - (port | length) - 1] -%}
{%- if a in ('0.0.0.0', '[::]', '*', '') -%}
{{ parts[0] }}/{{ a }}:{{ port }}
{%- else -%}
{{ parts[0] }}/{{ a | mask_ip }}:{{ port }}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
================================================================================
LISTENING SOCKET SWEEP
Generated {{ report_generated }} UTC
{% if report_mask %}
MASKED COPY. Bind addresses are partially redacted for distribution.
Wildcard binds are shown in full, deliberately: on this report they are the
finding, not the detail.
{% else %}
UNMASKED COPY. Contains full bind addresses. Do not forward.
{% endif %}
================================================================================

SUMMARY
  Hosts in scope    {{ host_rows | length }}
  Swept             {{ host_rows | selectattr('reachable') | list | length }}
  Not swept         {{ host_rows | rejectattr('reachable') | list | length }}

  Checks PASS       {{ host_row_counts.PASS }}
  Checks FAIL       {{ host_row_counts.FAIL }}
  Checks SKIP       {{ host_row_counts.SKIP }}

  A SKIP is NOT a pass. It means the socket list could not be compared, and
  what is listening on that host is unknown rather than approved.

{% for row in host_rows | sort(attribute='name') %}
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
{{ hostname(row.name) }}  [{{ row.verdict }}]
{% if not row.reachable %}
  NOT SWEPT -- {{ row.reason | default('no session established') }}
{#- No counts here. "0 unexpected listeners" for a host nobody reached
    renders as a measurement and reads as the cleanest machine in the fleet.
    See docs/gotchas.md entry 3. -#}
  No socket was examined on this host. What it is listening on is unknown,
  not approved.
{% else %}
{% for check in row.checks %}
  {{ '%-6s' | format(check.status) }} {{ addr(check.check.split(':', 1)[1] | default(check.check)) }}  ({{ check.check.split(':', 1)[0] }})
{%- if check.status != 'PASS' %}
           expected: {{ addr(check.expected | default('')) }}
           actual:   {{ addr(check.actual | default('')) }}
{% if check.reason | default('') %}           reason:   {{ check.reason | trim }}
{% endif %}
{%- endif %}
{% else %}
  No checks were produced for this host.
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
{#- Both sections below derive from host_rows, NOT from any pre-joined summary
    list. A flat list of "host :: finding" strings would have the name and
    address already baked in and would bypass both macros. That exact leak
    happened once in this repository; it is not repeated here. -#}
{% if host_row_counts.SKIP | int > 0 %}

UNEVALUATED
{% for row in host_rows | sort(attribute='name') %}
{% if not row.reachable %}
  {{ hostname(row.name) }} :: host -- {{ row.reason | default('no session established') }}
{% endif %}
{% for check in row.checks | selectattr('status', 'eq', 'SKIP') %}
  {{ hostname(row.name) }} :: {{ addr(check.check.split(':', 1)[1] | default(check.check)) }}
{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% if host_row_counts.FAIL | int > 0 %}

FAILED
{% for row in host_rows | sort(attribute='name') %}
{% for check in row.checks | selectattr('status', 'eq', 'FAIL') %}
  {{ hostname(row.name) }} :: {{ check.check.split(':', 1)[0] }} :: {{ addr(check.check.split(':', 1)[1] | default(check.check)) }}
{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
